  "step_3": "The decision reason requires: (1) identifying the reference value (zero, since no sure outcome exists), (2) computing the differences between outcomes and the reference value (i.e., 6000 and 3000), and (3) weighting these differences by their probabilities to compute a mean difference, then preferring the lottery with the more favorable (higher) weighted mean difference. In this case, both lotteries have the same expected value (6 Euros), so the reason would be indifferent. However, the individual does not mention expected value, differences from a reference, or any weighted comparison. Instead, the individual states that the 0.01% difference is negligible and that one would rather win more money — indicating a preference for higher outcome size, not for a higher weighted mean difference. The individual’s reasoning is based on outcome magnitude and ignores the probabilistic weighting of differences. Therefore, the individual did not apply the decision reason. The core elements of the reason (differences from reference, weighted by probabilities) are absent from the report. The justification is based on a different principle — maximizing outcome size, not on a difference-based expected value comparison. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
